  "query_preview": "..\nToday: Gusty winds today will lead to near lake wind conditions. \nTherefore a lake wind Advisory is out. However, RTMA which is much\nhigher in locations is not very supportive of hourly NBM wind \ngrids for today and they also do not appear to be matching up very\nwell with observations across the area, so stepped in an provided\na HRRR/ARW/NAMNest blend of CAMs models. Current Observations\nsuggest that winds will continue to to stay up through the\nafternoon befoe slackening. So, have extended the Lake Wind \nAdvisory through 6PM.\n\nTonight through Thursday: Ridging out west will sway back and \nforth across the region today through Thursday with a weak cold \nfront possibly showing up Tuesday and blunting the ridge to the \nsouth briefly. Otherwise, conditions will remain dry through \nthese periods.\n\nThursday night into Friday night: ridge will move east a a \nPacific Northwest trough moves into the region and turns the flow \naloft south. A cold frontal passage on friday may end up being \nthe focus for rain showers friday night into Saturday as pacific \nmoisture overruns the cold air. The strength of this frontal\npassage is also suggestive of strong winds at least in the \ncategory Lake Wind, but also possibly high wind. Uncertainty of \nthe frontal passage timing is strong as well, and this period will\nbe monitored for future changes.\n\nSaturday onward: Expect Temps back to near normal with isolated \nchances for showers from here on. But ensemble consistency does \nbreak down at this point onward.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\nNBM was largely used in support of the going forecast cycle. \nHowever, a CAMs model blend was used as a nudge to near term winds\nthrough 9PM. \n\nEnsemble consensus appears to break down around Saturday night.\nFrontal timing on Friday is currently in question. This has \nproduced a near 10 degree range for highs for the afternoon \nFriday which lowers confidence from this point onward. GAH",
